# Let the Darkness Depart! - Part 2
Tamil title: இருளகல! - பகுதி 2

The picture shows that there are not only the hills and craters mentioned in the story, but seas as well.

Nature has long hidden countless wonders of this kind within herself. Humanity acts with the determination that, somehow, they must all be discovered.

Even the story records only the final message sent by those who went to the moon; it does not say that they returned home victorious.

Many have failed while attempting to discover nature's secrets; many have been sacrificed; many have found only a portion of the truth. Yet the endeavour has not been abandoned. It continues.

Today there is even confidence that we shall ultimately discover the ages, conditions, and qualities through which scattered rays emerged from vast darkness, ordered sounds from immense noise, and order from great confusion.

Humanity possesses such power: the power to conquer nature, to control nature, and to understand nature's heart. The old idea that nature is humanity's enemy is changing.

Humanity gains courage and strength to stand against nature. Humans can do all this: sound the ocean's depths, travel through space, study the planets, and discover nature's secrets.

Yet although humanity can learn so much, human beings still have not succeeded in understanding one another. What are we to say of this wonder?

A great thinker laments that humans have learnt to fly like birds and swim like fish, but have not yet fully learnt to behave like human beings. Why?

Perhaps because the old darkness, old clamour, and old confusion have not been uprooted from the human mind.

Thus people must continually drive away not only the darkness outside but the darkness within themselves. I say "continually" because it cannot be expelled once and for all. Even while great success is being achieved in driving it away, darkness may slowly enter again from another direction.

To find an object lying in darkness, a lamp must be lit. Once the object is found, the lamp may be extinguished, but it must remain ready to burn when needed again. More important than all this, one's sight must remain unimpaired.

If the eye belongs to the face, the inner self too needs an eye. That eye is pure thought. Freedom from the thought, "For us, for ourselves alone; even what belongs to another must be ours; let it be seized from another for us; let another be destroyed for us," produces purity of mind. To become finer still, the thought must be, "For others; even if we have none, for others; for everyone."

There were times when Tamils possessed such purity of heart. This festival seems to remind us of it. Harshness disappears and cheerful fellowship abounds. We feel moved to say, "He, she, and everyone are our own." We wish that everyone should possess everything. What causes such a pure heart to arise on this festival day?

After vast darkness, immense noise, and great confusion had been transformed, humanity's first effort and first victory in bringing nature under its direction was agriculture. The farmers' festival is the new day of Pongal. Hard wasteland became cultivated field. Agriculture prospered so greatly that one could say one became a thousand: what was sown by the basketful was harvested by the cartload.

Humans who had hunted and lived like animals began building homes and practising domestic virtue only through this agricultural victory.

How many efforts were required before the age when humans competed with great wing-beating eagles could give way to an age when they placed parrots in cages, fed them kovvai fruit, and delighted in them! Humanity passed through dangers that make the heart tremble merely to contemplate. It suffered defeats and endured afflictions.

In forests still roamed by blood-mouthed wolves, wild boars, buffaloes, frightened deer, and murderous tigers, humans once lived as two-legged creatures eating what they killed. Today they have attained the level of governing countries, cultivating the arts and civilisation, and establishing universities.

Animals have not been able to change their nature of leaping, attacking, tearing, eating, hiding, and pouncing. No need for change arose, nor was an environment created that would permit change.

Humans, however, not only changed animal life and instinct but reconstructed their surroundings to suit that transformation. The new environment also enables human qualities to be refined further. If anyone dares depart from humanity, moral teachers exist to condemn them and government exists to punish them. Poor animals have none of these. They still possess only dense forests, waterfall banks, swamps, caves, and hollows.

When an animal always lives beside a thicket, will it develop the habit of calling others, "Come, sit down," instead of hiding and then pouncing? When it has no other means to appease hunger, can it adopt a method of asking to be given food rather than killing and eating? Animals therefore remain as they were: no need, opportunity, or path for changing their nature has arisen.

We must think about how to save ourselves from animals' actions. We cannot go to them and ask, "Animals, see how much we have changed! We have altered our instincts and become good. Why have you alone still not abandoned the habit of drinking blood?"

If animals possessed a way to express their thoughts, would they not instead ask us: "Human being, despite having every facility and opportunity, why do you at times return to something worse than animal nature?" Is that not true? We ourselves reveal it when speaking about others:

He pecks like an eagle;
he hisses like a snake;
he stings like a scorpion;
he shrieks like an owl;
he behaves like a monkey;
he is treacherous like a cat;
he is a brute like a buffalo bull;
he croaks like a frog;
he bores into one like a beetle;
there lies the donkey;
beat and chase away the dog;
he has the nature of a fox;
he springs like a tiger;
his back is like a camel's;
his disposition is a wolf's;
he steals like a rat;
he roars like a bear;
he moves like a tortoise;
he grips like a monitor lizard.

What do all these expressions reveal? Animal nature lies hidden in human beings like a snake in an anthill. It may raise its head at any time. We must always remain vigilant.

An animal shut in a cage cannot emerge and endanger others. But the animal nature within a human being is no caged beast. The room called the mind has neither bolt nor door. Animal instinct may therefore burst out at any moment.

We hear: he struck him; he cut him; he stabbed him; he tore him away; he split him; he broke him; he knocked him down; he killed him; he buried him; he destroyed his eye; he cut off his ear.

What do such reports tell us? That a human being can do everything an animal does, once did so, and, if vigilance lapses, can do so again. A beast that is uncaged and has not abandoned killing lurks within the human heart. The injuries and dangers inflicted upon humans by people within the country who have not destroyed their brutality far exceed the dangers posed by wild animals to people in the country.

In a forest we proceed fearfully, choosing our time and path. In a country, people move freely and trustingly. In that unguarded condition, if a human becomes a pouncing tiger, a rearing cobra, a stinging scorpion, or a pecking eagle, what will become of other people? The thought itself makes one tremble.

Why has humanity still not wholly escaped animal instinct? Humans emerged from the darkness in which they once dwelt, but darkness repeatedly attempts to imprison them again. That is why animal nature within humanity has not died.

Moral treatises were composed and governments established so that human animal instinct would not erupt and bring great harm to others.

When it appears in some people at certain times, government intervenes to punish them. The underlying idea is that others, seeing this, will restrain themselves because animal instinct will not be permitted expression. But punishment alone is insufficient. Government must take great care and responsibility to reshape the environment from time to time so that animal instinct does not arise in human beings.

Valluvar says that a government failing to assume such responsibility is a burden upon the earth:

> The cruel sceptre gathers the unlearned around itself;
> no other burden weighs upon the earth like that.

Despotic rule gathers the ignorant as its fortress; nothing else burdens the earth so greatly. That is the meaning.

There are mountains and trees upon the earth, humans and animals, including harmful beasts and crawling creatures. Valluvar did not call them burdens. He called the rule of the unlearned a burden upon the earth.

It is government's duty to punish thieves; no one denies it. But the responsibility of good government is to reform the conditions in which people are compelled to survive through theft and consequently commit crime.

When ancient Tamil kings heeded the words of moral teachers, they carefully created conditions in which evils such as theft, murder, and rape would not arise. If people committed crimes under their rule, those kings understood that although the criminals deserved punishment, the rulers themselves deserved condemnation.

There is evidence that not merely the kings but the people too possessed such responsibility. That condition is absent today, and the very nature of crime has greatly expanded.

"Because people lack love for others, the world is filled with thefts, frauds, and disorder.

"Thieves occasionally steal at night in the darkness of the new moon. But there is no limit to the thefts committed openly in broad daylight before everyone. Those who, through greed for wealth, swear falsely, give false evidence, create false documents, bring fraudulent suits, borrow beyond their means, refuse to repay debts, charge unjust interest, seize property entrusted to them, take wages without working, extract labour from the poor without paying wages, pass invalid currency, use false measures and weights, charge excessive prices, collect the price of good merchandise and supply bad goods, seek wealth through gambling, fail to return found property to its owners, envy another's possessions, buy stolen goods, aid theft, teach others to steal, cause others to lose property, possess wealth but give no alms to the poor, or take money in exchange for giving a woman in marriage are all thieves who plunder in broad daylight.

"Moreover, rulers who impose unbearable and ruinous taxes upon the poor, taxes on country, forest, house, cattle, goats, votes, tickets, associations, songs, uplands, pollution, roads, coats, spears, whips, and so forth, and thereby produce disorder, are unjust.

"Some heads of courts who accept bribes and decide for one side are cruel. Some legal officers who eagerly wait for quarrels to arise in families and lawsuits to arrive are deceitful. Some physicians who wish for fevers, diarrhoea, broken heads, and fractured limbs, and who cultivate disease as one raises a child so that they may live by it, are hardened, deceitful thieves!"

Thus did Vedanayagam Pillai speak in anguish.

Good government must accept responsibility not only for ending theft caused by want but also for preventing daylight robberies committed to increase what one already possesses. Otherwise the fool will be caught while the rogue lives triumphantly. That will ruin the social order, create doubt about whom government exists to serve, and destroy the trust and respect people must place in government.

Once that trust collapses, the country becomes a building whose foundation has broken. Vast darkness, immense noise, and great confusion will march forth demanding victims, and they will obtain them.

Today I enjoy the fruits of service rendered by those who lived before me. Many things supporting my life were created by others in earlier times. Since I enjoy them, I must repay the debt by serving so that those who come after me may receive and benefit from the fruit of my labour. That is the quality of gratitude. Everyone must work according to the opportunity available to them, guided by this awareness.

Those who do not attempt this are as good as nonexistent.

A child grows by drinking her mother's milk. When she becomes a girl and her mother suffers illness, is it not proper that she remain beside her and serve? If instead, while the mother's body wastes away amid coughing, wheezing, and laboured breath, the girl thinks without concern, "My mother can no longer protect me; why should I care about her?" and goes on playing, would not the womb that bore her burn with anguish, and would not onlookers' eyes blaze?

In the same way, everyone must serve the world that gave them life, add colour to it, and enrich it. Otherwise they are a burden upon the earth. What else could they be?

People of little understanding ask whether pure service is performed merely to earn fame. Fame does not remain attached to its recipient, nor does it die with that person. It endures afterward and becomes an ornament to the country.

Valluvar instructs those who acquire wealth to give it and rejoice. He calls such a life one lived with honour. Giving and living with honour are strongly commended. Rebuking those who do not follow this path as burdens upon the earth, he says:

> Men who hoard their gains and desire no honour:
> their appearance is a burden upon the earth.

The people must therefore hold the ideal of becoming ornaments to the earth rather than burdens upon it. Only such people are fit to establish good government.

Today it is the people who form the government. Whether good government is established lies in their hands. This precious opportunity comes only once in five years. If they fail to use it properly, they must suffer for the next five.

On the cherished festival celebrated with joy once each year, this must be borne in mind and a sound decision made.

Every day the people see vast darkness depart when the rising sun appears.

The Rising Sun dispels the vast darkness. The Rising Sun helped transform the condition of immense noise and great confusion and make the world a home for living beings. The Rising Sun also became the firm support by which agriculture prospered.

That the Rising Sun deserves praise and reverence and gives prosperity is a great truth acclaimed by all.

On the new day of Pongal, which is the auspicious day for praising that Rising Sun, we remember how vast darkness withdrew, immense noise ceased, and great confusion subsided; how the world became a dwelling place; how humans left the animal condition, formed countries, and established governments.

I wrote this piece, "Let the Darkness Depart," to appeal to those with knowledge and responsibility to labour courageously, through the establishment of good government, to remove animal instinct so that it may not remain rooted in human beings in the days to come.

This work has taken the form of a collection containing many subjects precisely to emphasise that each condition and idea found here ought to be treated more extensively and clearly by competent writers.

---NOTES---
This file is the direct continuation and conclusion of `irulagala.md`; its Tamil scan begins without a repeated title. The English title therefore identifies it as Part 2 rather than inventing a separate source title. `உதயசூரியன்` is rendered as `Rising Sun`, retaining both the natural image and its political resonance. The two Tirukkural passages are translated in context rather than assigned modern couplet numbering. The long ethical catalogue is explicitly attributed in the source to Vedanayagam Pillai. A stray final OCR token, `ஆஷ்`, absent from the closing scan, was removed from the corrected Tamil source.
